### PRIORITY 4: Integrated Urgent Care — Enhanced NHS 111 and Clinical Assessment Services (CAS)

**Definition:** Strengthening NHS 111 through enhanced Clinical Assessment Services (CAS), integration with GP out-of-hours, mental health crisis lines, pharmacy, and ambulance services to provide comprehensive remote clinical assessment and reduce inappropriate 999 calls and A&E attendances.

| Criterion | Score | Rationale |
|-----------|-------|-----------|
| Magnitude of winter problem | 5 | NHS 111 handles approximately 20 million calls annually; winter surge significantly strains capacity. Poor integration leads to unnecessary 999 calls and A&E attendances. |
| Strength of evidence | 3 | Evidence from NHSE integrated urgent care evaluation (2019) and Kings Fund analysis. Association of Ambulance Chief Executives data. However, causal evidence from high-quality studies limited. Evidence for CAS effectiveness improving outcomes is still developing. |
| Cost-effectiveness | 4 | Diverting calls from 999/A&E represents significant cost savings if effective. 999 call costs ~£250; A&E attendance ~£150-200 (NHS reference costs). However, safety concerns about undertriage need balancing. |
| NHS Plan alignment | 5 | Core component of NHS Urgent and Emergency Care transformation. NHSE Integrated Urgent Care Service Specification. |
| Short/medium/long-term impact | 4 | Short-term: Enhancement of existing infrastructure feasible within 1-3 years. Requires workforce and IT investment. |
| Health inequalities reduction | 3 | Access to 111 requires phone/internet access. Language barriers. However, 111 is more equitable than GP access in some respects. |
| **Composite Score** | **73/100** | **High priority** |

