### PRIORITY 6: Early Detection and Management of Cardiovascular Disease (CVD) Including Heart Failure

**Definition:** Population-level CVD risk assessment, early identification and management of hypertension, atrial fibrillation (AF), and heart failure to prevent acute cardiovascular events (MI, stroke, acute heart failure) that drive winter emergency admissions.

| Criterion | Score | Rationale |
|-----------|-------|-----------|
| Magnitude of winter problem | 5 | CVD is the leading cause of mortality and a major driver of emergency admissions year-round with winter excess. AF-related stroke, acute coronary syndromes, and decompensated heart failure all have winter peaks. NHS estimates ~3.4 million people with undiagnosed hypertension. |
| Strength of evidence | 5 | Hypertension treatment: Strong RCT evidence (SPRINT, HOPE-3, multiple meta-analyses). AF screening and anticoagulation: NICE NG196 (strong evidence). Heart failure management: NICE NG106 (strong guideline base). Blood pressure check programme: NHS evidence base. |
| Cost-effectiveness | 5 | Prevention of stroke (mean cost £14,000+ per episode) and MI through antihypertensive/anticoagulation therapy is among the most cost-effective interventions in medicine. QALY gains well-established. |
| NHS Plan alignment | 5 | NHS CVD Prevention Programme. NHS Long Term Plan CVD ambitions (prevent 150,000 heart attacks and strokes over 10 years). NHS Health Checks programme. |
| Short/medium/long-term impact | 4 | Short-term: Health check expansion and AF detection campaigns feasible. Medium-term: Requires GP capacity for follow-up. Long-term: Population-level impact on emergency admissions takes time. |
| Health inequalities reduction | 5 | CVD disproportionately affects South Asian, Black African/Caribbean, and deprived populations. Targeted CVD prevention can directly address health inequalities. NHS Health Checks equity data demonstrates differential uptake issues to address. |
| **Composite Score** | **89/100** | **Very High priority** |

