### PRIORITY 11: Primary Care Access Transformation — Same-Day Access and Advanced Practitioner Roles

**Definition:** Redesign of primary care access through same-day telephonic/digital triage (e.g., total triage model), deployment of clinical pharmacists, physiotherapists, paramedics, and physician associates within PCNs to increase access, reduce GP workload pressure, and prevent unnecessary A&E attendances.

| Criterion | Score | Rationale |
|-----------|-------|-----------|
| Magnitude of winter problem | 4 | Difficulty accessing GP drives significant A&E attendance. NHS surveys consistently show 30-40% of people who attend A&E tried to see GP first. Winter GP demand spikes significantly. |
| Strength of evidence | 3 | Total triage (e.g., Klinik, AccuRx systems): Service evaluation evidence, limited high-quality RCTs. Advanced practitioner roles: Growing evidence base — NIHR evidence synthesis on physiotherapist-led triage; pharmacist roles in PCNs supported by NHSE evaluation. Overall: Moderate evidence, implementation heterogeneous. |
| Cost-effectiveness | 4 | Workforce substitution models generally cost-effective. Avoiding A&E attendances (~£150-200 each) has clear value. However, potential for activity generation and demand inducement to reduce cost-effectiveness. |
| NHS Plan alignment | 5 | Core to NHS primary care recovery plan. ARRS (Additional Roles Reimbursement Scheme). PCN DES. NHS Delivery Plan for Primary Care (2023). |
| Short/medium/long-term impact | 4 | Short-term: ARRS already operational — scaling feasible. Medium-term: Training and integration takes time. |
| Health inequalities reduction | 3 | Digital triage risks disadvantaging older and deprived populations without internet/smartphone access. Requires hybrid models. Language access critical. |
| **Composite Score** | **73/100** | **Medium-High priority** |

