### PRIORITY 12: Type 2 Diabetes Prevention and Early Management Programme

**Definition:** Structured National Diabetes Prevention Programme (NDPP) expansion, enhanced HbA1c screening in high-risk populations, and early intensive management of Type 2 diabetes to prevent complications (acute hyperglycaemia, hyperosmolar states, cardiovascular events, foot infections) driving winter admissions.

| Criterion | Score | Rationale |
|-----------|-------|-----------|
| Magnitude of winter problem | 4 | Diabetic foot disease, hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic states, and DKA contribute significantly to winter admissions. T2D-related CVD events compound winter pressure. ~4.3 million people diagnosed with diabetes in UK. |
| Strength of evidence | 5 | NDPP effectiveness: Strong RCT evidence (Diabetes Prevention Program, USA; adapted evidence for UK). NHS DPP evaluation: NHSE evaluation shows reductions in T2D progression. SMBG and structured education: NICE evidence base. Remission through dietary intervention: DiRECT trial (Lean et al., Lancet 2018) — high-quality UK RCT. |
| Cost-effectiveness | 5 | NDPP: NICE-endorsed, cost-effective. Preventing T2D complications has very strong cost-effectiveness evidence. DiRECT-based remission programmes: Cost-effective modelling (NHS data). |
| NHS Plan alignment | 5 | NHS Long Term Plan: 200,000 places on NDPP annually. NICE guideline NG28. NHS diabetes transformation programme. |
| Short/medium/long-term impact | 3 | Prevention programmes have medium-long term impact on admissions. Short-term: Acute management improvements feasible. Long-term impact on winter pressures through complication reduction. |
| Health inequalities reduction | 4 | T2D disproportionately affects South Asian, Black African/Caribbean communities and deprived populations. Culturally tailored NDPP versions needed. NHS data shows uptake gaps. |
| **Composite Score** | **78/100** | **High priority** |

