### PRIORITY 14: Ambulance Service Transformation — Hear and Treat / See and Treat and Alternative Pathways

**Definition:** Reduction of unnecessary conveyance to hospital by ambulance services through enhanced Hear and Treat (telephone clinical assessment) and See and Treat (on-scene treatment and discharge) pathways, including co-responder models and direct referral to alternative care settings.

| Criterion | Score | Rationale |
|-----------|-------|-----------|
| Magnitude of winter problem | 4 | Ambulance conveyance to A&E drives significant ED overcrowding. Winter sees surge in 999 calls. Many conveyances are of patients who do not require emergency hospital care. Ambulance response time performance has been a major winter crisis indicator. |
| Strength of evidence | 3 | See and Treat models: Evidence from JRCALC (Joint Royal Colleges Ambulance Liaison Committee) guidance. Systematic review evidence moderate. Specific outcome data variable by region. Safety of non-conveyance: Some evidence (Turner et al., systematic review). Direct GP referral from ambulance: Limited but emerging evidence. |
| Cost-effectiveness | 4 | Avoiding unnecessary conveyance + ED attendance saves significant costs. Ambulance dispatch cost ~£250; adding ED attendance ~£150. However, underconveyance safety risks are real and costly in litigation terms. |
| NHS Plan alignment | 4 | NHS Urgent and Emergency Care Recovery Plan. NHSE Ambulance Improvement Programme. |
| Short/medium/long-term impact | 3 | Workforce training and protocol development: 1-3 years for incremental improvements. Fundamental redesign: 3-5 years. |
| Health inequalities reduction | 3 | Geographic variation in ambulance response times disproportionately affects rural and deprived urban communities. Alternative pathways may not be equally accessible. |
| **Composite Score** | **67/100** | **Medium priority** |

