### PRIORITY 15: Environmental Sustainability in Healthcare Delivery (Green Pharmacy, Sustainable Anaesthesia, Supply Chain)

**Definition:** Systematic reduction of the NHS's carbon footprint through green pharmacy (transition from high-GWP inhalers to dry powder inhalers, reducing overprescription), sustainable anaesthesia (desflurane elimination), and supply chain decarbonisation, while improving patient outcomes.

| Criterion | Score | Rationale |
|-----------|-------|-----------|
| Magnitude of winter problem | 2 | Indirect link — climate change drives worsening winters and respiratory conditions (air quality, cold snaps). However, direct impact on winter pressures is indirect and longer-term. |
| Strength of evidence | 4 | Metered-dose inhaler to dry powder inhaler switch: Evidence that DPIs have equivalent clinical efficacy and significantly lower carbon footprint (Wilkinson et al., BMJ 2019 — though verify details). Desflurane: RCP/AAGBI guidance on desflurane phaseout has strong evidence base. NHS Greener NHS programme evidence is developing. |
| Cost-effectiveness | 3 | Green pharmacy (inhaler switch): May be cost-saving as DPIs often cheaper. Others: Variable; some sustainability investments have upfront costs. |
| NHS Plan alignment | 4 | NHS Net Zero target (2045 for supply chain, 2040 for direct emissions). Greener NHS programme. Life Sciences sector has sustainability commitments. |
| Short/medium/long-term impact | 2 | Limited short-term impact on winter pressures specifically. Medium-long term: Climate health co-benefits. |
| Health inequalities reduction | 3 | Air quality improvements from NHS sustainability disproportionately benefit deprived communities (who live closer to pollution sources). Asthma prevalence equity dimensions. |
| **Composite Score** | **56/100** | **Lower priority for winter focus** |

