#### Intervention 1.1: Annual Influenza Vaccination for High-Risk Groups and Healthcare Workers

**Implementation Level:** Policy + Clinical

**Mechanism:** Reduces influenza infection rates and severity in high-risk groups (elderly, immunocompromised, chronic disease patients); reduces staff sickness absence; prevents influenza-related pneumonia and exacerbations of underlying respiratory disease

**Resources Required:**
- **Low-Medium:** Vaccine procurement (national contract); GP/pharmacy delivery infrastructure already exists; additional community outreach for underserved groups requires moderate additional resource (community health workers, mobile units)

**Implementation Requirements:**
- Maintained national procurement and distribution infrastructure
- GP/pharmacy clinical systems for recall
- Active outreach to low-uptake groups (ethnic minorities, homeless, Gypsy/Traveller communities)
- Healthcare worker vaccination programme with occupational health delivery

**Strength of Evidence:** **STRONG — Level 1**
- Cochrane systematic review (Jefferson et al., 2010, multiple updates) on influenza vaccination in elderly: reduces hospitalisation for influenza/pneumonia by ~40-60% in vaccine-matched seasons
- JCVI annual recommendations based on systematic evidence review
- PHE/UKHSA seasonal vaccine effectiveness monitoring provides annual real-world evidence

*Jefferson T, Di Pietrantonj C, Al-Ansary LA, et al. Vaccines for preventing influenza in the elderly. Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2010;(2):CD004876. [I am confident this series of Cochrane reviews exists; users should verify most recent version/year]*

**Feasibility:** **Within 1-3 years** — already operational; requires expansion to underserved groups

**Barriers:**
- Vaccine hesitancy (particularly post-COVID)
- Access barriers for marginalised groups
- Supply chain variability in pandemic years
- HCW vaccination rates still below 80% target in many trusts

**Metrics:**
- Flu vaccination coverage rate by risk group and deprivation quintile
- Influenza-related A&E attendance rates (winter)
- Staff sickness absence rates (respiratory cause)
- Hospitalisation rate for influenza-coded admissions (ICD-10 J10-J11)

**Evidence Category: Strong evidence**

