#### Intervention 3.4: Anticipatory Care Prescribing and Just-in-Case Medication for End-of-Life Patients

**Implementation Level:** Clinical

**Mechanism:** Prescribing anticipatory medications ("just-in-case" boxes — typically for pain, agitation, secretions, nausea) for patients in the last year of life enables community-based management of acute symptom crises, preventing unnecessary emergency admissions and enabling people to die in their preferred place; reduces winter A&E pressure from dying patients

**Resources Required:**
- **Low-Medium:** GP prescribing; community nursing to administer; patient/family education; storage systems; palliative care team input for complex cases

**Implementation Requirements:**
- Identification of patients in last year of life (Gold Standards Framework prognostic indicator guidance)
- Anticipatory prescription by GP with community nurse authority to administer
- Patient/family education about when and how to access
- 24/7 community nursing availability to support

**Strength of Evidence:** **MODERATE**
- Bowers B, et al. Anticipatory prescribing in community palliative care: a systematic review. Palliat Med. 2019;33(9):1052–1060. [I have moderate confidence — verify details]
- NICE NG31 (Care of dying adults in the last days of life, 2015)
- NHS RightCare palliative care data: Significant variation in place of death
- **Caveat:** Specific outcome data on A&E reduction from anticipatory prescribing limited

**Feasibility:** **Within 1-3 years** — relatively low resource; requires community nursing capacity

**Barriers:**
- Community nursing availability (especially 24/7)
- GP identification of patients in last year of life (often occurs late)
- Family anxiety and reluctance to use medications
- Rural access to community nursing

**Metrics:**
- Proportion of deaths in preferred place (NHS Outcomes Framework)
- Emergency admissions in last 30 days of life
- Proportion of known dying patients with anticipatory medications prescribed
- A&E attendances in last year of life

**Evidence Category: Moderate evidence; promising and undervalued** 
**Undervalued note:** Anticipatory prescribing is relatively low-cost and aligns with patient preference data showing >70% wish to die at home, yet community nursing infrastructure is often inadequate to support it.

